{"id":"2026-11-22-ENPH","date":"2026-11-22","ticker":"ENPH","name":"Enphase Energy","sector":"Information Technology","industry":"Solar","price":19.63,"price_date":"2026-11-20","timeframe_days":182,"headline":"Enphase's sales fell 29% in September, the outlook points to about a 30% drop in December, and US rooftop solar demand is shrinking after the tax credit ended","side":"short","timeframe":"About 6 months, through the next two quarterly reports","thesis":"Enphase's sales fell 29% from a year ago in the September quarter, worse than the 20% drops in the two quarters before, and the December guide of $225M to $255M is about 30% below a year ago. Even that guide leans on about $51M of one-time sales to installers buying ahead to qualify for tax rules (safe harbor). Without it, December sales would be near $190M. US sales, three-quarters of the total, fell 33%. The cause is the end of the home solar tax credit (25D) and high interest rates: the CEO said sell-through to customers fell 35% from a year ago and home solar permits are down about 35% too. He also said sell-through will fall about 10% from September to December, and that September already missed his own plan. Gross margin fell from 60% to 43%. This is a business in a deep slump with no floor in sight yet.","wrong_if":"A close above 26 would retake the level the stock gapped down from after the October 27 report, and mean the selling is over. The idea is also wrong if December sales beat the $255M top of the guide, or if Congress restores a home solar tax credit.","business":"Enphase makes microinverters, small boxes that sit under each solar panel on a roof and turn the panel's power into the kind homes use. It also sells home batteries, EV chargers and software that manages it all. Most sales go to solar installers and distributors, about three-quarters in the US. It is starting to sell to small commercial buildings with a new product (IQ9).","segments_note":"US sales were $233M in September, down 33%, and the drop has gotten worse each quarter, from flat a year ago to -11%, -16% and now -33%. International sales were $60M, about flat after falling by roughly half a year earlier, and Europe is shifting to battery sales, about half of the region. Commercial sell-through grew about 50% from June, but from a small base.","growth_note":"Sales were $293M, down 29%, after -20% in June. Gross margin fell to 43.3% from 60% in June, and operating margin fell to 2.2% from 17.7%, so profit is shrinking faster than sales. September also included $85M of safe harbor sales that will drop to about $51M in December. Missing: the brief's guidance table has no entries for the last two calls, so the guide numbers here come from the call text.","commentary":[{"point":"December quarter sales expected at $225M to $255M, including about $51M of safe harbor sales, with 100 to 120 megawatt-hours of batteries.","speaker":"Mandy Yang, CFO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"Sell-through fell 35% from a year ago due to higher interest rates and the end of the 25D tax credit, and home solar permits were down about 35%.","speaker":"Badrinarayanan Kothandaraman, CEO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"Expect global sell-through to fall about 10% from September to December, roughly $20M.","speaker":"Badrinarayanan Kothandaraman, CEO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"December gross margin expected at 38% to 41%, down from 43.3% in September.","speaker":"Mandy Yang, CFO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"number"},{"point":"Admitted September sell-through grew only 5% from June against his own plan of about 10%, so revenue landed near the low end of the guide.","speaker":"Badrinarayanan Kothandaraman, CEO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"tone"},{"point":"Still sounded upbeat about batteries, expecting about 50% growth in 2027 and a long-term goal of 40% of the US market.","speaker":"Badrinarayanan Kothandaraman, CEO","call":"Q3 2026","kind":"tone"}],"tone_note":"Tone barely moved: net tone fell about 5 points, hedging rose about 6 and there was one dodge. The words stayed upbeat about batteries and new products while the numbers kept getting worse, so this idea leans on the numbers, not the tone.","technical_note":"The stock closed at 19.63, right at its 52-week low of 19.47 and far under its 50-day average (30.44) and 200-day average (39.97). It has fallen 37% in a month, including a 16% drop on October 28 after the report, and is down 73% from its 52-week high of 73.74. RSI is 25, which is deeply oversold. Volume over the last 20 days is 37% above normal.","valuation_note":"At 29.7 times trailing earnings, Enphase is far below its median of 75 since 2020, but its earnings are falling fast, so a low multiple on shrinking earnings is not cheap. If profit keeps shrinking, the multiple will look higher at the same price.","levels":{"entry_lo":19,"entry_hi":23,"stop":26,"target":10,"note":"Sell short between 19 and 23, best on a bounce toward 22 or 23 given how oversold the stock is. The stop at 26 is above the October 27 gap and about three daily ranges above the posted price. The target of 10 assumes sales keep falling into 2027. From the posted price that is about $10 of downside for $6 of risk, or 1.5 to 1."},"risks":["Squeeze risk is high: RSI is 25 and the stock is at its lows, so a short-covering rally could take it back to the mid-20s quickly.","Many investors short solar stocks, which makes squeezes more likely when news turns.","Lower interest rates or a new tax credit could revive demand fast.","The battery and commercial businesses are growing and could surprise.","Not known from the brief: short interest, the date of the next report, and the December 2025 quarter details used for the year-ago comparison."],"catalysts":["The December quarter report, likely early February 2027, against the $225M to $255M guide.","Federal Reserve rate decisions, since home solar loans depend on rates.","Monthly US solar permit and installer data.","Any new federal or state solar incentives."],"facts":{"financials":{"updated":"2026-11-18","adj_eps_name":null,"quarters":[{"q":"Dec '23","id":"FY2023Q4","end":"2023-12-31","rev":302.57,"gm":48.5,"om":-3.4,"nm":6.9,"eps":0.15,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-58.2,"eps_yoy":-85.8},{"q":"Mar '24","id":"FY2024Q1","end":"2024-03-31","rev":263.339,"gm":43.9,"om":-11.1,"nm":-6.1,"eps":-0.12,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-63.7,"eps_yoy":-111.8},{"q":"Jun '24","id":"FY2024Q2","end":"2024-06-30","rev":303.458,"gm":45.2,"om":0.6,"nm":3.6,"eps":0.08,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-57.3,"eps_yoy":-92.7},{"q":"Sep '24","id":"FY2024Q3","end":"2024-09-30","rev":380.873,"gm":46.8,"om":13.1,"nm":12.0,"eps":0.33,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-30.9,"eps_yoy":-58.8},{"q":"Dec '24","id":"FY2024Q4","end":"2024-12-31","rev":382.713,"gm":51.8,"om":14.3,"nm":16.2,"eps":0.41,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":26.5,"eps_yoy":173.3},{"q":"Mar '25","id":"FY2025Q1","end":"2025-03-31","rev":356.084,"gm":47.2,"om":9.0,"nm":8.3,"eps":0.22,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":35.2,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Jun '25","id":"FY2025Q2","end":"2025-06-30","rev":363.153,"gm":46.9,"om":10.2,"nm":10.2,"eps":0.28,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":19.7,"eps_yoy":250.0},{"q":"Sep '25","id":"FY2025Q3","end":"2025-09-30","rev":410.427,"gm":47.8,"om":16.1,"nm":16.2,"eps":0.5,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":7.8,"eps_yoy":51.5},{"q":"Dec '25","id":"FY2025Q4","end":"2025-12-31","rev":343.321,"gm":44.3,"om":6.5,"nm":11.3,"eps":0.28,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-10.3,"eps_yoy":-31.7},{"q":"Mar '26","id":"FY2026Q1","end":"2026-03-31","rev":282.9,"gm":35.5,"om":-10.5,"nm":-2.6,"eps":-0.06,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-20.6,"eps_yoy":-127.3},{"q":"Jun '26","id":"FY2026Q2","end":"2026-06-30","rev":291.854,"gm":60.0,"om":17.7,"nm":12.4,"eps":0.27,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-19.6,"eps_yoy":-3.6},{"q":"Sep '26","id":"FY2026Q3","end":"2026-09-30","rev":293.329,"gm":43.3,"om":2.2,"nm":8.0,"eps":0.17,"adj_eps":null,"rev_yoy":-28.5,"eps_yoy":-66.0}]},"segments":[{"label":"Geography","quarters":["Dec '23","Mar '2